s on creating memories rather than  obtaining more items this tends to be a  lot more fulfilling and it leads me to  reason number four increased happiness  minimalism goes hand-in-hand with  living in which we learned to take a  step back and enjoy the small details of  life that were often too busy to notice  and take for granted for example instead  of chugging a coffee before going to  work taking the cup and actually feeling  the worth in your hands smelling the  aroma as you take it feeling the warmth  as it's going down your throat and while  all of this is going on actually  noticing what's going on  the noise of the city is it birds  singing of one breathes what is the last  time you've actually enjoyed a cup of  coffee this way a lot of the times we  end up so busy that we end up living  life kind of mindlessly what are you  guys think of slow living let me know in  the comments below  not only can slow living increase  happiness but minimalism places a  greater focus on having and doing the  things that bring you joy this brings  you to have much more appreciation for  the things that you have and it leads me  to the next reason number 5 gratitude  I've mentioned this one in my previous  video how to become more optimistic but  minimalism sets the stage for optimism  this is because you start to realize all  the things that you have both tangible  and intangible that you're fortunate to  have often times  the more we appreciate the things that  we do have for example I used to have a  lot of decorations in my home and a lot  of artwork that was purchased  store-bought now I only decorate with  family photos  and paintings and artwork that their  iron  this might not appear beautiful to  somebody else but to me they hold a  greater value and they bring me joy  because they actually have memories  attached to them  number six newly found hobbies and more  creativity as humans if we are made in  the image and likeness of a create war  then it means we are meant to be  creative you don't have to teach a child  how to be creative they just are we were  once that way but we lost it in the  midst of the frenzy and chaos of life  there's slow and intentional living our  new experiences often linked to newly  found hobbies and more creativity  because we don't stop learning I'm not  sure where I've heard this so if anybody  knows please let me know in the comments  below and this is we start dying when we  stop learning and this brings me to  reason number seven improved health  studies show that our brains begin to  deteriorate when we stop learning new  things and stretching our minds with new  experiences by creating new memories and  reducing the stress in your life you're  not only more likely to be happier  but healthier as well as I've mentioned  in a previous video stress is often the  cause of disease because it  there's inflammation which triggers  illness so to recap minimalism can  decrease stress which makes you  healthier which makes you happier it's a  win-win all around number eight  saving money minimalism can save you so  much money  iron minimalism journey actually started  with Dave Ramsey because we wanted to  get out of debt we weren't exactly  looking for a simplified lifestyle  this kind of kick-started my desire to  be financially free and to have a life  that wasn't so chaotic that wasn't so  busy I hadn't actually noticed that I  was following several minimalist  practices until several months into that  money saving journey in some ways yes  you do end up spending more money  upfront but you end up saving in the  long term for example I ended up buying  cloth sponges and cop napkins for our  kitchen yes they were a bit expensive  because I wanted a little bit better  quality but I've not had to purchase  those things the number two years now  another example before our first child  was born we ended up spending roughly  five hundred dollars on cloth diapers  which seemed like an insane amount at  